(; constants used by Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic, declared with their translated types ;)

(; doc: Commutative version of `AddCancelMonoid`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddCancelCommMonoid ;)
AddCancelCommMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An additive commutative group is an additive group with commutative `(+)`.  [Wikidata Q181296](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q181296) ;)
(; //LEAN:AddCommGroup ;)
AddCommGroup : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An additive commutative monoid is an additive monoid with commutative `(+)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddCommMonoid ;)
AddCommMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Units of an `AddMonoid`, bundled version.  An element of an `AddMonoid` is a unit if it has a two-sided additive inverse. This version bundles the inverse element so that it can be computed. For a predicate see `isAddUnit`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddUnits ;)
AddUnits : (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: The bot (`⊥`, `\bot`) element  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `⊥` in identifiers is `bot`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Bot.bot ;)
Bot__bot : Elem X.

(; doc: Commutative version of `CancelMonoid`. ;)
(; //LEAN:CancelCommMonoid ;)
CancelCommMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A commutative group is a group with commutative `(*)`.  [Wikidata Q181296](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q181296) ;)
(; //LEAN:CommGroup ;)
CommGroup : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A commutative monoid is a monoid with commutative `(*)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:CommMonoid ;)
CommMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Set / lattice complement  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `ᶜ` in identifiers is `compl`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Compl.compl ;)
Compl__compl : (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: A `Bool` (that is, `decide p`) paired with a proof of `p` if `decide p` is `true` or a proof of `¬p` if `decide p` is `false`. The easiest way to extract these proofs is to use `of_decide_eq_true` or `of_decide_eq_false`.  `Decidable` instances are primarily used via `if`-expressions and the tactic `decide`. In conditional expressions, the `Decidable` instance for the proposition is used to select a branch. At run time, this case distinction code is identical to that which would be generated for a `Bool`-based conditional. In proofs, the tactic `decide` synthesizes an instance of `Decidable p`, attempts to reduce `decide p` to `true`, and then succeeds with the proof `of_decide_eq_true rfl` if it can.  Because `Decidable` carries data, when writing `@[simp]` lemmas which include a `Decidable` instance on the LHS, it is best to use `{_ : Decidable p}` rather than `[Decidable p]` so that non-canonical instances can be found via unification rather than instance synthesis. ;)
(; //LEAN:Decidable ;)
Decidable : (p : Prop) -> Prop.

(; doc: Two elements of a lattice are disjoint if their inf is the bottom element.   (This generalizes disjoint sets, viewed as members of the subset lattice.)  Note that we define this without reference to `⊓`, as this allows us to talk about orders where the infimum is not unique, or where implementing `Inf` would require additional `Decidable` arguments. ;)
(; //LEAN:Disjoint ;)
Disjoint : (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mp : α → β` is the induced "cast" operation, mapping elements of `α` to elements of `β`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mp` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mp ;)
Eq__mp : (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem Y.

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mpr : β → α` is the induced "cast" operation in the reverse direction, mapping elements of `β` to elements of `α`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mpr` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mpr ;)
Eq__mpr : (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> Elem X.

(; doc: Non-dependent recursor for the equality type. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.ndrec ;)
Eq__ndrec : (m : Elem (motive a)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> Elem (motive b).

(; doc: `Eq.refl a : a = a` is reflexivity, the unique constructor of the equality type. See also `rfl`, which is usually used instead. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.refl ;)
Eq__refl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

(; doc: Equality is symmetric: if `a = b` then `b = a`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have a variable `h : a = b`, `h.symm` can be used as shorthand for `Eq.symm h` as a proof of `b = a`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.symm ;)
Eq__symm : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> Proof (Eqt X b a).

(; doc: Equality is transitive: if `a = b` and `b = c` then `a = c`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have variables or expressions `h₁ : a = b` and `h₂ : b = c`, you can use `h₁.trans h₂ : a = c` as shorthand for `Eq.trans h₁ h₂`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.trans ;)
Eq__trans : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt X b c)) -> Proof (Eqt X a c).

(; doc: `α ≃ β` is the type of functions from `α → β` with a two-sided inverse. ;)
(; //LEAN:Equiv ;)
Equiv : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: Inverse of an equivalence `e : α ≃ β`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Equiv.symm ;)
Equiv__symm : (e : Elem (Equiv X Y)) -> Elem (Equiv Y X).

(; doc: Convert an `α ≃ β` to `α ↪ β`.  This is also available as a coercion `Equiv.coeEmbedding`. The explicit `Equiv.toEmbedding` version is preferred though, since the coercion can have issues inferring the type of the resulting embedding. For example:  ```lean -- Works: example (s : Finset (Fin 3)) (f : Equiv.Perm (Fin 3)) : s.map f.toEmbedding = s.map f := by simp -- Error, `f` has type `Fin 3 ≃ Fin 3` but is expected to have type `Fin 3 ↪ ?m_1 : Type ?` example (s : Finset (Fin 3)) (f : Equiv.Perm (Fin 3)) : s.map f = s.map f.toEmbedding := by simp ``` ;)
(; //LEAN:Equiv.toEmbedding ;)
Equiv__toEmbedding : (f : Elem (Equiv X Y)) -> Elem (Function__Embedding X Y).

(; doc: An ordered additive monoid with one-sided 'subtraction' in the sense that if `a ≤ b`, then there is some `c` for which `a + c = b`. This is a weaker version of the condition on canonical orderings defined by `CanonicallyOrderedAdd`. ;)
(; //LEAN:ExistsAddOfLE ;)
ExistsAddOfLE : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `Finset α` is the type of finite sets of elements of `α`. It is implemented as a multiset (a list up to permutation) which has no duplicate elements. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset ;)
Finset : (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: The property `s.Nonempty` expresses the fact that the finset `s` is not empty. It should be used in theorem assumptions instead of `∃ x, x ∈ s` or `s ≠ ∅` as it gives access to a nice API thanks to the dot notation. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.Nonempty ;)
Finset__Nonempty : (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `attach s` takes the elements of `s` and forms a new set of elements of the subtype `{x // x ∈ s}`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.attach ;)
Finset__attach : (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> Elem (Finset (Subtype (x => Membership__mem s x))).

(; doc: `Finset.biUnion s t` is the union of `t a` over `a ∈ s`.  (This was formerly `bind` due to the monad structure on types with `DecidableEq`.) ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.biUnion ;)
Finset__biUnion : (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (t : (Elem X -> Finset Y)) -> Elem (Finset Y).

(; doc: `s.card` is the number of elements of `s`, aka its cardinality.  The notation `#s` can be accessed in the `Finset` locale. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.card ;)
Finset__card : (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> Elem Nat.

(; doc: `cons a s h` is the set `{a} ∪ s` containing `a` and the elements of `s`. It is the same as `insert a s` when it is defined, but unlike `insert a s` it does not require `DecidableEq α`, and the union is guaranteed to be disjoint. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.cons ;)
Finset__cons : (a : Elem X) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (h : Proof (not (Membership__mem s a))) -> Elem (Finset X).

(; //LEAN:Finset.decidableMem ;)
Finset__decidableMem : (a : Elem X) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> Proof (Decidable (Membership__mem s a)).

(; doc: Given a finite set `s`, the diagonal, `s.diag` is the set of pairs of the form `(a, a)` for `a ∈ s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.diag ;)
Finset__diag : (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> Elem (Finset (Prod X X)).

(; doc: Disjoint sum of finsets. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.disjSum ;)
Finset__disjSum : (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (t : Elem (Finset Y)) -> Elem (Finset (Sum X Y)).

(; doc: `disjUnion s t h` is the set such that `a ∈ disjUnion s t h` iff `a ∈ s` or `a ∈ t`. It is the same as `s ∪ t`, but it does not require decidable equality on the type. The hypothesis ensures that the sets are disjoint. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.disjUnion ;)
Finset__disjUnion : (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (t : Elem (Finset X)) -> (h : Proof (Disjoint s t)) -> Elem (Finset X).

(; //LEAN:Finset.disjUnion_eq_union ;)
Finset__disjUnion_eq_union : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (t : Elem (Finset X)) -> (h : Proof (Disjoint s t)) -> Proof (Eqt (Finset X) (Finset__disjUnion s t h) (Union__union s t)).

(; doc: `disjiUnion s f h` is the set such that `a ∈ disjiUnion s f` iff `a ∈ f i` for some `i ∈ s`. It is the same as `s.biUnion f`, but it does not require decidable equality on the type. The hypothesis ensures that the sets are disjoint. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.disjiUnion ;)
Finset__disjiUnion : (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (t : (Elem X -> Finset Y)) -> (hf : Proof (Set__PairwiseDisjoint s t)) -> Elem (Finset Y).

(; doc: `erase s a` is the set `s - {a}`, that is, the elements of `s` which are not equal to `a`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.erase ;)
Finset__erase : (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem (Finset X).

(; doc: `Finset.filter p s` is the set of elements of `s` that satisfy `p`.  For example, one can use `s.filter (· ∈ t)` to get the intersection of `s` with `t : Set α` as a `Finset α` (when a `DecidablePred (· ∈ t)` instance is available). ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.filter ;)
Finset__filter : (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> Elem (Finset X).

(; //LEAN:Finset.filter_subset ;)
Finset__filter_subset : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> Proof (Set__Subset (Finset__filter (x1 => p x1) s) s).

(; doc: `fold op b f s` folds the commutative associative operation `op` over the `f`-image of `s`, i.e. `fold (+) b f {1,2,3} = f 1 + f 2 + f 3 + b`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.fold ;)
Finset__fold : (op : (Elem Y -> Elem Y -> Y)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> Elem Y.

(; //LEAN:Finset.fold_cons ;)
Finset__fold_cons : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (op : (Elem Y -> Elem Y -> Y)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Commutative op)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Associative op)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (not (Membership__mem s a))) -> Proof (Eqt Y (Finset__fold op b f (Finset__cons a s h)) (op (f a) (Finset__fold op b f s))).

(; //LEAN:Finset.fold_image ;)
Finset__fold_image : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (op : (Elem Y -> Elem Y -> Y)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Commutative op)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Associative op)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> (g : (Elem Z -> X)) -> (s : Elem (Finset Z)) -> (H : Proof (Set__InjOn g s)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (Finset__fold op b f (Finset__image g s)) (Finset__fold op b (Function__comp f g) s)).

(; //LEAN:Finset.fold_insert ;)
Finset__fold_insert : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (op : (Elem Y -> Elem Y -> Y)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Commutative op)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Associative op)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (not (Membership__mem s a))) -> Proof (Eqt Y (Finset__fold op b f (Insert__insert a s)) (op (f a) (Finset__fold op b f s))).

(; //LEAN:Finset.fold_op_distrib ;)
Finset__fold_op_distrib : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (op : (Elem Y -> Elem Y -> Y)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Commutative op)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Associative op)) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x : Elem Y) -> (x1 : Elem Y) -> Proof (Eqt Y (Finset__fold op (op x x1) (x2 => op (f x2) (g x2)) s) (op (Finset__fold op x f s) (Finset__fold op x1 g s))).

(; //LEAN:Finset.fold_singleton ;)
Finset__fold_singleton : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (op : (Elem Y -> Elem Y -> Y)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Commutative op)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Associative op)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Y (Finset__fold op b f (Singleton__singleton a)) (op (f a) b)).

(; //LEAN:Finset.fold_union_inter ;)
Finset__fold_union_inter : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (op : (Elem Y -> Elem Y -> Y)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Commutative op)) -> (_ : Proof (Std__Associative op)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x1 : Elem (Finset X)) -> (x2 : Elem (Finset X)) -> (x3 : Elem Y) -> (x4 : Elem Y) -> Proof (Eqt Y (op (Finset__fold op x3 f (Union__union x1 x2)) (Finset__fold op x4 f (Inter__inter x1 x2))) (op (Finset__fold op x4 f x1) (Finset__fold op x3 f x2))).

(; doc: `image f s` is the forward image of `s` under `f`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.image ;)
Finset__image : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> Elem (Finset Y).

(; doc: When `f` is an embedding of `α` in `β` and `s` is a finset in `α`, then `s.map f` is the image finset in `β`. The embedding condition guarantees that there are no duplicates in the image. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.map ;)
Finset__map : (f : Elem (Function__Embedding X Y)) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> Elem (Finset Y).

(; //LEAN:Finset.mem_filter ;)
Finset__mem_filter : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (iff (Membership__mem (Finset__filter (x1 => p x1) s) a) (and (Membership__mem s a) (p a))).

(; //LEAN:Finset.mem_image_of_mem ;)
Finset__mem_image_of_mem : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Membership__mem s a)) -> Proof (Membership__mem (Finset__image f s) (f a)).

(; //LEAN:Finset.mem_sdiff._simp_1 ;)
Finset__mem_sdiff___simp_1 : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (t : Elem (Finset X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (Membership__mem (SDiff__sdiff s t) a) (and (Membership__mem s a) (not (Membership__mem t a)))).

(; //LEAN:Finset.mem_union ;)
Finset__mem_union : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (t : Elem (Finset X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (iff (Membership__mem (Union__union s t) a) (or (Membership__mem s a) (Membership__mem t a))).

(; //LEAN:Finset.mem_univ ;)
Finset__mem_univ : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Fintype X)) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Proof (Membership__mem Finset__univ x1).

(; //LEAN:Finset.mem_univ._simp_1 ;)
Finset__mem_univ___simp_1 : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Fintype X)) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (Membership__mem Finset__univ x1) True).

(; doc: `∏ x ∈ s, f x` is the product of `f x` as `x` ranges over the elements of the finite set `s`.  When the index type is a `Fintype`, the notation `∏ x, f x`, is a shorthand for `∏ x ∈ Finset.univ, f x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.prod ;)
Finset__prod : (s : Elem (Finset I)) -> (f : (Elem I -> M)) -> Elem M.

(; //LEAN:Finset.prod_const_one ;)
Finset__prod_const_one : (I : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (s : Elem (Finset I)) -> (_ : Proof (CommMonoid M)) -> Proof (Eqt M (Finset__prod s (_x => 1)) 1).

(; doc: `range n` is the set of natural numbers less than `n`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.range ;)
Finset__range : (n : Elem Nat) -> Elem (Finset Nat).

(; //LEAN:Finset.sdiff_union_self_eq_union ;)
Finset__sdiff_union_self_eq_union : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (t : Elem (Finset X)) -> Proof (Eqt (Finset X) (Union__union (SDiff__sdiff s t) t) (Union__union s t)).

(; //LEAN:Finset.subset_union_left ;)
Finset__subset_union_left : (X : Set) -> (x1 : Elem (Finset X)) -> (x2 : Elem (Finset X)) -> Proof (Set__Subset x1 (Union__union x1 x2)).

(; //LEAN:Finset.subset_univ ;)
Finset__subset_univ : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Fintype X)) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> Proof (Set__Subset s Finset__univ).

(; doc: Given a finset `s` and a predicate `p`, `s.subtype p` is the finset of `Subtype p` whose elements belong to `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.subtype ;)
Finset__subtype : (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> Elem (Finset (Subtype (x1 => p x1))).

(; doc: `∑ x ∈ s, f x` is the sum of `f x` as `x` ranges over the elements of the finite set `s`.  When the index type is a `Fintype`, the notation `∑ x, f x`, is a shorthand for `∑ x ∈ Finset.univ, f x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.sum ;)
Finset__sum : (s : Elem (Finset I)) -> (f : (Elem I -> M)) -> Elem M.

(; //LEAN:Finset.sup_eq_union ;)
Finset__sup_eq_union : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (Finset X)) -> (t : Elem (Finset X)) -> Proof (Eqt (Finset X) (Union__union s t) (Union__union s t)).

(; doc: Given a finset of elements `α ⊕ β`, extract all the elements of the form `α`. This forms a quasi-inverse to `disjSum`, in that it recovers its left input.  See also `List.partitionMap`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.toLeft ;)
Finset__toLeft : (u : Elem (Finset (Sum X Y))) -> Elem (Finset X).

(; doc: Given a finset of elements `α ⊕ β`, extract all the elements of the form `β`. This forms a quasi-inverse to `disjSum`, in that it recovers its right input.  See also `List.partitionMap`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.toRight ;)
Finset__toRight : (u : Elem (Finset (Sum X Y))) -> Elem (Finset Y).

(; //LEAN:Finset.union_comm ;)
Finset__union_comm : (X : Set) -> (x1 : Elem (Finset X)) -> (x2 : Elem (Finset X)) -> Proof (Eqt (Finset X) (Union__union x1 x2) (Union__union x2 x1)).

(; doc: `univ` is the universal finite set of type `Finset α` implied from the assumption `Fintype α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.univ ;)
Finset__univ : Elem (Finset X).

(; doc: The underlying multiset ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.val ;)
Finset__val : (self : Elem (Finset X)) -> Elem (Multiset X).

(; doc: `Fintype α` means that `α` is finite, i.e. there are only finitely many distinct elements of type `α`. The evidence of this is a finset `elems` (a list up to permutation without duplicates), together with a proof that everything of type `α` is in the list. ;)
(; //LEAN:Fintype ;)
Fintype : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Embedding of a `Subtype`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Embedding.subtype ;)
Function__Embedding__subtype : (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> Elem (Function__Embedding (Subtype (x => p x)) X).

(; doc: g factors through f : `f a = f b → g a = g b` ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.FactorsThrough ;)
Function__FactorsThrough : (g : (Elem X -> Z)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function `f : α → β` is called injective if `f x = f y` implies `x = y`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Injective ;)
Function__Injective : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: Function composition, usually written with the infix operator `∘`. A new function is created from two existing functions, where one function's output is used as input to the other.  Examples:  * `Function.comp List.reverse (List.drop 2) [3, 2, 4, 1] = [1, 4]`  * `(List.reverse ∘ List.drop 2) [3, 2, 4, 1] = [1, 4]`  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∘` in identifiers is `comp`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.comp ;)
Function__comp : (f : (Elem Y -> W)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem W.

(; doc: The constant function that ignores its argument.  If `a : α`, then `Function.const β a : β → α` is the “constant function with value `a`”. For all arguments `b : β`, `Function.const β a b = a`. It is often written directly as `fun _ => a`.  Examples:  * `Function.const Bool 10 true = 10`  * `Function.const Bool 10 false = 10`  * `Function.const String 10 "any string" = 10` ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.const ;)
Function__const : (Y : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (x : Elem Y) -> Elem X.

(; doc: Extension of a function `g : α → γ` along a function `f : α → β`.  For every `a : α`, `f a` is sent to `g a`. `f` might not be surjective, so we use an auxiliary function `j : β → γ` by sending `b : β` not in the range of `f` to `j b`. If you do not care about the behavior outside the range, `j` can be used as a junk value by setting it to be `0` or `Classical.arbitrary` (assuming `γ` is nonempty).  This definition is mathematically meaningful only when `f a₁ = f a₂ → g a₁ = g a₂` (spelled `g.FactorsThrough f`). In particular this holds if `f` is injective.  A typical use case is extending a function from a subtype to the entire type. If you wish to extend `g : {b : β // p b} → γ` to a function `β → γ`, you should use `Function.extend Subtype.val g j`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.extend ;)
Function__extend : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Z)) -> (j : (Elem Y -> Z)) -> (x : Elem Y) -> Elem Z.

(; doc: `a • b` computes the product of `a` and `b`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent, but it is intended to be used for left actions.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `•` in identifiers is `smul`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HSMul.hSMul ;)
HSMul__hSMul : (x1 : Elem X) -> (x2 : Elem Y) -> Elem x.

(; doc: **Alias** of `not_congr`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Iff.not ;)
Iff__not : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> Proof (iff (not a) (not b)).

(; //LEAN:Iff.trans ;)
Iff__trans : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (c : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (iff b c)) -> Proof (iff a c).

(; doc: `default` is a function that produces a "default" element of any `Inhabited` type. This element does not have any particular specified properties, but it is often an all-zeroes value. ;)
(; //LEAN:Inhabited.default ;)
Inhabited__default : Elem X.

(; doc: `insert x xs` inserts the element `x` into the collection `xs`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Insert.insert ;)
Insert__insert : (x1 : Elem x) -> (x2 : Elem Z) -> Elem Z.

(; doc: The absolute value of an integer is its distance from `0`.  This function is overridden by the compiler with an efficient implementation. This definition is the logical model.  Examples:  * `(7 : Int).natAbs = 7`  * `(0 : Int).natAbs = 0`  * `(-11 : Int).natAbs = 11` ;)
(; //LEAN:Int.natAbs ;)
Int__natAbs : (m : Elem Int) -> Elem Nat.

(; doc: `a ∩ b` is the intersection of `a` and `b`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∩` in identifiers is `inter`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Inter.inter ;)
Inter__inter : (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: An element `a : M` of an `AddMonoid` is an `AddUnit` if it has a two-sided additive inverse. The actual definition says that `a` is equal to some `u : AddUnits M`, where `AddUnits M` is a bundled version of `IsAddUnit`. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsAddUnit ;)
IsAddUnit : (a : Elem M) -> Prop.

(; doc: Two elements `x` and `y` are complements of each other if `x ⊔ y = ⊤` and `x ⊓ y = ⊥`. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsCompl ;)
IsCompl : (x2 : Elem X) -> (y : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: If `x` and `y` are to be complementary in an order, they should be disjoint. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsCompl.disjoint ;)
IsCompl__disjoint : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (PartialOrder X)) -> (_ : Proof (BoundedOrder X)) -> (x2 : Elem X) -> (y : Elem X) -> (self : Proof (IsCompl x2 y)) -> Proof (Disjoint x2 y).

(; //LEAN:IsCompl.sup_eq_top ;)
IsCompl__sup_eq_top : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Lattice X)) -> (_ : Proof (BoundedOrder X)) -> (x2 : Elem X) -> (y : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (IsCompl x2 y)) -> Proof (Eqt X (Max__max x2 y) Top__top).

(; //LEAN:IsCompl.symm ;)
IsCompl__symm : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (PartialOrder X)) -> (_ : Proof (BoundedOrder X)) -> (x2 : Elem X) -> (y : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (IsCompl x2 y)) -> Proof (IsCompl y x2).

(; doc: An element `a : M` of a `Monoid` is a unit if it has a two-sided inverse. The actual definition says that `a` is equal to some `u : Mˣ`, where `Mˣ` is a bundled version of `IsUnit`. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsUnit ;)
IsUnit : (a : Elem M) -> Prop.

(; doc: `LT α` is the typeclass which supports the notation `x < y` where `x y : α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LT ;)
LT : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Linked lists: ordered lists, in which each element has a reference to the next element.  Most operations on linked lists take time proportional to the length of the list, because each element must be traversed to find the next element.  `List α` is isomorphic to `Array α`, but they are useful for different things: * `List α` is easier for reasoning, and `Array α` is modeled as a wrapper around `List α`. * `List α` works well as a persistent data structure, when many copies of the tail are shared. When   the value is not shared, `Array α` will have better performance because it can do destructive   updates. ;)
(; //LEAN:List ;)
List : (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: The list has no duplicates: it contains every element at most once.  It is defined as `Pairwise (· ≠ ·)`: each element is unequal to all other elements. ;)
(; //LEAN:List.Nodup ;)
List__Nodup : (x : Elem (List X)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:List.brecOn ;)
List__brecOn : (t : Elem (List X)) -> (F_1 : ((t1 : Elem (List X)) -> Elem (List__below t1) -> motive t1)) -> Elem (motive t).

(; doc: Counts the number of times an element occurs in a list.  Examples: * `[1, 1, 2, 3, 5].count 1 = 2` * `[1, 1, 2, 3, 5].count 5 = 1` * `[1, 1, 2, 3, 5].count 4 = 0` ;)
(; //LEAN:List.count ;)
List__count : (a : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem (List X)) -> Elem Nat.

(; doc: The length of a list.  This function is overridden in the compiler to `lengthTR`, which uses constant stack space.  Examples: * `([] : List String).length = 0` * `["green", "brown"].length = 2` ;)
(; //LEAN:List.length ;)
List__length : (x : Elem (List X)) -> Elem Nat.

(; doc: Applies a function to each element of the list, returning the resulting list of values.  `O(|l|)`.  Examples: * `[a, b, c].map f = [f a, f b, f c]` * `[].map Nat.succ = []` * `["one", "two", "three"].map (·.length) = [3, 3, 5]` * `["one", "two", "three"].map (·.reverse) = ["eno", "owt", "eerht"]` ;)
(; //LEAN:List.map ;)
List__map : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (l : Elem (List X)) -> Elem (List Y).

(; doc: Computes the product of the elements of a list.  Examples: * `[a, b, c].prod = a * (b * (c * 1))` * `[1, 2, 5].prod = 10` ;)
(; //LEAN:List.prod ;)
List__prod : (x2 : Elem (List X)) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:List.prod_toFinset._f ;)
List__prod_toFinset___f : (I : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (CommMonoid M)) -> (f : (Elem I -> M)) -> (x2 : Elem (List I)) -> (f1 : Elem (List__below x2)) -> (h : Proof (List__Nodup x2)) -> Proof (Eqt M (Finset__prod (List__toFinset x2) (x3 => f x3)) (List__prod (List__map f x2))).

(; doc: Computes the sum of the elements of a list.  Examples: * `[a, b, c].sum = a + (b + (c + 0))` * `[1, 2, 5].sum = 8` ;)
(; //LEAN:List.sum ;)
List__sum : (x2 : Elem (List X)) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `toFinset l` removes duplicates from the list `l` to produce a finset. ;)
(; //LEAN:List.toFinset ;)
List__toFinset : (l : Elem (List X)) -> Elem (Finset X).

(; doc: The membership relation `a ∈ s : Prop` where `a : α`, `s : γ`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∈` in identifiers is `mem`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Membership.mem ;)
Membership__mem : (x1 : Elem Z) -> (x2 : Elem x) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function `f` is monotone if `a ≤ b` implies `f a ≤ f b`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Monotone ;)
Monotone : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `Multiset α` is the quotient of `List α` by list permutation. The result is a type of finite sets with duplicates allowed. ;)
(; //LEAN:Multiset ;)
Multiset : (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: The cardinality of a multiset is the sum of the multiplicities of all its elements, or simply the length of the underlying list. ;)
(; //LEAN:Multiset.card ;)
Multiset__card : (x : Elem (Multiset X)) -> Elem Nat.

(; //LEAN:Multiset.card_bind ;)
Multiset__card_bind : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (s : Elem (Multiset X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Multiset Y)) -> Proof (Eq (Multiset__card (Multiset__bind s f)) (Multiset__sum (Multiset__map (Function__comp Multiset__card f) s))).

(; doc: `count a s` is the multiplicity of `a` in `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Multiset.count ;)
Multiset__count : (a : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem (Multiset X)) -> Elem Nat.

(; doc: `map f s` is the lift of the list `map` operation. The multiplicity of `b` in `map f s` is the number of `a ∈ s` (counting multiplicity) such that `f a = b`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Multiset.map ;)
Multiset__map : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (Multiset X)) -> Elem (Multiset Y).

(; //LEAN:Multiset.map_const ;)
Multiset__map_const : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (s : Elem (Multiset X)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> Proof (Eqt (Multiset Y) (Multiset__map (Function__const X b) s) (Multiset__replicate (Multiset__card s) b)).

(; doc: Product of a multiset given a commutative monoid structure on `M`. `prod {a, b, c} = a * b * c` ;)
(; //LEAN:Multiset.prod ;)
Multiset__prod : (x1 : Elem (Multiset M)) -> Elem M.

(; //LEAN:Multiset.prod_dvd_prod_of_dvd ;)
Multiset__prod_dvd_prod_of_dvd : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (_ : Proof (CommMonoid N)) -> (S : Elem (Multiset M)) -> (g1 : (Elem M -> N)) -> (g2 : (Elem M -> N)) -> (h : Proof (forall M (a => if (Membership__mem S a) (divide (g1 a) (g2 a))))) -> Proof (divide (Multiset__prod (Multiset__map g1 S)) (Multiset__prod (Multiset__map g2 S))).

(; //LEAN:Multiset.prod_replicate ;)
Multiset__prod_replicate : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (CommMonoid M)) -> (n : Elem Nat) -> (a : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (Multiset__prod (Multiset__replicate n a)) (pow a n)).

(; doc: `replicate n a` is the multiset containing only `a` with multiplicity `n`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Multiset.replicate ;)
Multiset__replicate : (n : Elem Nat) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem (Multiset X).

(; doc: Sum of a multiset given a commutative additive monoid structure on `M`. `sum {a, b, c} = a + b + c` ;)
(; //LEAN:Multiset.sum ;)
Multiset__sum : (x1 : Elem (Multiset M)) -> Elem M.

(; //LEAN:Multiset.sum_map_tsub ;)
Multiset__sum_map_tsub : (I : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid M)) -> (_ : Proof (PartialOrder M)) -> (_ : Proof (ExistsAddOfLE M)) -> (_ : Proof (OrderedSub M)) -> (l : Elem (Multiset I)) -> (f : (Elem I -> M)) -> (g : (Elem I -> M)) -> (hfg : Proof (forall I (x4 => if (Membership__mem l x4) (Leq (g x4) (f x4))))) -> Proof (Eqt M (Multiset__sum (Multiset__map (x3 => minus (f x3) (g x3)) l)) (minus (Multiset__sum (Multiset__map f l)) (Multiset__sum (Multiset__map g l)))).

(; doc: `toFinset s` removes duplicates from the multiset `s` to produce a finset. ;)
(; //LEAN:Multiset.toFinset ;)
Multiset__toFinset : (s : Elem (Multiset X)) -> Elem (Finset X).

(; //LEAN:Or.resolve_left ;)
Or__resolve_left : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (or a b)) -> (na : Proof (not a)) -> Proof b.

(; doc: An order is an `OrderBot` if it has a least element. We state this using a data mixin, holding the value of `⊥` and the least element constraint. ;)
(; //LEAN:OrderBot ;)
OrderBot : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `OrderedSub α` means that `α` has a subtraction characterized by `a - b ≤ c ↔ a ≤ c + b`. In other words, `a - b` is the least `c` such that `a ≤ b + c`.  This is satisfied both by the subtraction in additive ordered groups and by truncated subtraction in canonically ordered monoids on many specific types. ;)
(; //LEAN:OrderedSub ;)
OrderedSub : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A partial order is a reflexive, transitive, antisymmetric relation `≤`. ;)
(; //LEAN:PartialOrder ;)
PartialOrder : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: The product type, usually written `α × β`. Product types are also called pair or tuple types. Elements of this type are pairs in which the first element is an `α` and the second element is a `β`.  Products nest to the right, so `(x, y, z) : α × β × γ` is equivalent to `(x, (y, z)) : α × (β × γ)`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `×` in identifiers is `Prod`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Prod ;)
Prod : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: Constructs a pair. This is usually written `(x, y)` instead of `Prod.mk x y`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `(a, b)` in identifiers is `mk`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Prod.mk ;)
Prod__mk : (fst : Elem X) -> (snd : Elem Y) -> Elem (Prod X Y).

(; doc: Quotient types coarsen the propositional equality for a type so that terms related by some equivalence relation are considered equal. The equivalence relation is given by an instance of `Setoid`.  Set-theoretically, `Quotient s` can seen as the set of equivalence classes of `α` modulo the `Setoid` instance's relation `s.r`. Functions from `Quotient s` must prove that they respect `s.r`: to define a function `f : Quotient s → β`, it is necessary to provide `f' : α → β` and prove that for all `x : α` and `y : α`, `s.r x y → f' x = f' y`. `Quotient.lift` implements this operation.  The key quotient operators are:  * `Quotient.mk` places elements of the underlying type `α` into the quotient.  * `Quotient.lift` allows the definition of functions from the quotient to some other type.  * `Quotient.sound` asserts the equality of elements related by `r`  * `Quotient.ind` is used to write proofs about quotients by assuming that all elements are    constructed with `Quotient.mk`.  `Quotient` is built on top of the primitive quotient type `Quot`, which does not require a proof that the relation is an equivalence relation. `Quotient` should be used instead of `Quot` for relations that actually are equivalence relations. ;)
(; //LEAN:Quotient ;)
Quotient : (s : Proof (Setoid X)) -> Set.

(; doc: Places an element of a type into the quotient that equates terms according to an equivalence relation.  The setoid instance is provided explicitly. `Quotient.mk'` uses instance synthesis instead.  Given `v : α`, `Quotient.mk s v : Quotient s` is like `v`, except all observations of `v`'s value must respect `s.r`. `Quotient.lift` allows values in a quotient to be mapped to other types, so long as the mapping respects `s.r`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Quotient.mk ;)
Quotient__mk : (s : Proof (Setoid X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem (Quotient s).

(; doc: `a \ b` is the set difference of `a` and `b`, consisting of all elements in `a` that are not in `b`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `\` in identifiers is `sdiff`. ;)
(; //LEAN:SDiff.sdiff ;)
SDiff__sdiff : (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `f` is bijective from `s` to `t` if `f` is injective on `s` and `f '' s = t`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.BijOn ;)
Set__BijOn : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (t : Elem (powerset Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: Given the set `s`, `Elem s` is the `Type` of element of `s`.  It is currently an abbreviation so that instance coming from `Subtype` are available. If you're interested in making it a `def`, as it probably should be, you'll then need to create additional instances (and possibly prove lemmas about them). See e.g. `Mathlib/Data/Set/Order.lean`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Elem ;)
Set__Elem : (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Set.

(; doc: `f` is injective on `s` if the restriction of `f` to `s` is injective. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.InjOn ;)
Set__InjOn : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: `MapsTo f s t` means that the image of `s` is contained in `t`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.MapsTo ;)
Set__MapsTo : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (t : Elem (powerset Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: The relation `r` holds pairwise on the set `s` if `r x y` for all *distinct* `x y ∈ s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Pairwise ;)
Set__Pairwise : (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (r : (Elem X -> Elem X -> Prop)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A set is `PairwiseDisjoint` under `f`, if the images of any distinct two elements under `f` are disjoint.  `s.Pairwise Disjoint` is (definitionally) the same as `s.PairwiseDisjoint id`. We prefer the latter in order to allow dot notation on `Set.PairwiseDisjoint`, even though the former unfolds more nicely. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.PairwiseDisjoint ;)
Set__PairwiseDisjoint : (s : Elem (powerset I)) -> (f : (Elem I -> X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: The subset relation on sets. `s ⊆ t` means that all elements of `s` are elements of `t`.  Note that you should **not** use this definition directly, but instead write `s ⊆ t`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Subset ;)
Set__Subset : (x : Elem (powerset X)) -> (x1 : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: The image of `s : Set α` by `f : α → β`, written `f '' s`, is the set of `b : β` such that `f a = b` for some `a ∈ s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.image ;)
Set__image : (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Elem (powerset Y).

(; //LEAN:Set.mem_toFinset ;)
Set__mem_toFinset : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (_ : Proof (Fintype (Set__Elem s))) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (iff (Membership__mem (Set__toFinset s) a) (Membership__mem s a)).

(; doc: Range of a function.  This function is more flexible than `f '' univ`, as the image requires that the domain is in Type and not an arbitrary Sort. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.range ;)
Set__range : (f : (Elem I -> X)) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: Construct a finset enumerating a set `s`, given a `Fintype` instance. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.toFinset ;)
Set__toFinset : (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Elem (Finset X).

(; doc: The universal set on a type `α` is the set containing all elements of `α`.  This is conceptually the "same as" `α` (in set theory, it is actually the same), but type theory makes the distinction that `α` is a type while `Set.univ` is a term of type `Set α`. `Set.univ` can itself be coerced to a type `↥Set.univ` which is in bijection with (but distinct from) `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.univ ;)
Set__univ : Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: A setoid is a type with a distinguished equivalence relation, denoted `≈`.  The `Quotient` type constructor requires a `Setoid` instance. ;)
(; //LEAN:Setoid ;)
Setoid : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `x ≈ y` is the distinguished equivalence relation of a setoid. ;)
(; //LEAN:Setoid.r ;)
Setoid__r : (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: `singleton x` is a collection with the single element `x` (notation: `{x}`).  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `{x}` in identifiers is `singleton`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Singleton.singleton ;)
Singleton__singleton : (x1 : Elem x) -> Elem Y.

(; doc: A _subsingleton_ is a type with at most one element. It is either empty or has a unique element.  All propositions are subsingletons because of proof irrelevance: false propositions are empty, and all proofs of a true proposition are equal to one another. Some non-propositional types are also subsingletons. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subsingleton ;)
Subsingleton : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: All the elements of a type that satisfy a predicate.  `Subtype p`, usually written `{ x : α // p x }` or `{ x // p x }`, contains all elements `x : α` for which `p x` is true. Its constructor is a pair of the value and the proof that it satisfies the predicate. In run-time code, `{ x : α // p x }` is represented identically to `α`.  There is a coercion from `{ x : α // p x }` to `α`, so elements of a subtype may be used where the underlying type is expected.  Examples:  * `{ n : Nat // n % 2 = 0 }` is the type of even numbers.  * `{ xs : Array String // xs.size = 5 }` is the type of arrays with five `String`s.  * Given `xs : List α`, `List { x : α // x ∈ xs }` is the type of lists in which all elements are    contained in `xs`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `{ x // p x }` in identifiers is `subtype`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subtype ;)
Subtype : (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> Set.

(; doc: A version of `x.property` or `x.2` where `p` is syntactically applied to the coercion of `x` instead of `x.1`. A similar result is `Subtype.mem` in `Mathlib/Data/Set/Basic.lean`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subtype.prop ;)
Subtype__prop : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (x : Elem (Subtype (x1 => p x1))) -> Proof (p x).

(; //LEAN:Subtype.val_injective ;)
Subtype__val_injective : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> Proof (Function__Injective p).

(; doc: The disjoint union of types `α` and `β`, ordinarily written `α ⊕ β`.  An element of `α ⊕ β` is either an `a : α` wrapped in `Sum.inl` or a `b : β` wrapped in `Sum.inr`. `α ⊕ β` is not equivalent to the set-theoretic union of `α` and `β` because its values include an indication of which of the two types was chosen. The union of a singleton set with itself contains one element, while `Unit ⊕ Unit` contains distinct values `inl ()` and `inr ()`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Sum ;)
Sum : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: Case analysis for sums that applies the appropriate function `f` or `g` after checking which constructor is present. ;)
(; //LEAN:Sum.elim ;)
Sum__elim : (f : (Elem X -> Z)) -> (g : (Elem Y -> Z)) -> (x : Elem (Sum X Y)) -> Elem Z.

(; doc: Left injection into the sum type `α ⊕ β`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Sum.inl ;)
Sum__inl : (val : Elem X) -> Elem (Sum X Y).

(; doc: Right injection into the sum type `α ⊕ β`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Sum.inr ;)
Sum__inr : (val : Elem Y) -> Elem (Sum X Y).

(; doc: Compose two proofs by transitivity, generalized over the relations involved. ;)
(; //LEAN:Trans.trans ;)
Trans__trans : (x : Elem (r a b)) -> (x1 : Elem (s b c)) -> Elem (t a c).

(; doc: `True` is a proposition and has only an introduction rule, `True.intro : True`. In other words, `True` is simply true, and has a canonical proof, `True.intro` For more information: [Propositional Logic](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/propositions_and_proofs.html#propositional-logic) ;)
(; //LEAN:True ;)
True : Prop.

(; doc: `a ∪ b` is the union of `a` and `b`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∪` in identifiers is `union`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Union.union ;)
Union__union : (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `Unique α` expresses that `α` is a type with a unique term `default`.  This is implemented as a type, rather than a `Prop`-valued predicate, for good definitional properties of the default term. ;)
(; //LEAN:Unique ;)
Unique : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Units of a `Monoid`, bundled version. Notation: `αˣ`.  An element of a `Monoid` is a unit if it has a two-sided inverse. This version bundles the inverse element so that it can be computed. For a predicate see `IsUnit`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Units ;)
Units : (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: `Xor a b` is the exclusive-or of propositions. ;)
(; //LEAN:Xor ;)
Xor : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:and_imp._simp_1 ;)
and_imp___simp_1 : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (c : Prop) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (if (and a b) c) (if a (if b c))).

(; //LEAN:by_cases ;)
by_cases : (p : Prop) -> (q : Prop) -> (hpq : Proof (if p q)) -> (hnpq : Proof (if (not p) q)) -> Proof q.

(; doc: Congruence in the function argument: if `a₁ = a₂` then `f a₁ = f a₂` for any (nondependent) function `f`. This is more powerful than it might look at first, because you can also use a lambda expression for `f` to prove that `<something containing a₁> = <something containing a₂>`. This function is used internally by tactics like `congr` and `simp` to apply equalities inside subterms.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congrArg ;)
congrArg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; doc: **Alias** of `congrArg`. ;)
(; //LEAN:congr_arg ;)
congr_arg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; //LEAN:disjoint_sdiff_self_left ;)
disjoint_sdiff_self_left : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (y : Elem X) -> (_ : Proof (GeneralizedBooleanAlgebra X)) -> Proof (Disjoint (SDiff__sdiff y x) x).

(; //LEAN:div_eq_of_eq_mul' ;)
div_eq_of_eq_mul' : (G : Set) -> (_ : Proof (CommGroup G)) -> (a : Elem G) -> (b : Elem G) -> (c : Elem G) -> (h : Proof (Eqt G a (times b c))) -> Proof (Eqt G (div a b) c).

(; //LEAN:eq_comm ;)
eq_comm : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Proof (iff (Eqt X a b) (Eqt X b a)).

(; //LEAN:eq_iff_eq_of_mul_eq_mul ;)
eq_iff_eq_of_mul_eq_mul : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (CancelCommMonoid X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (d : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X (times a b) (times c d))) -> Proof (iff (Eqt X a c) (Eqt X b d)).

(; //LEAN:forall_congr ;)
forall_congr : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (q : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (h : Proof (forall X (a2 => Eqt Prop (p a2) (q a2)))) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (forall X (a => p a)) (forall X (a1 => q a1))).

(; //LEAN:forall_const._simp_1 ;)
forall_const___simp_1 : (b : Prop) -> (X : Set) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (forall X (x => b)) b).

(; doc: The identity function. `id` takes an implicit argument `α : Sort u` (a type in any universe), and an argument `a : α`, and returns `a`.  Although this may look like a useless function, one application of the identity function is to explicitly put a type on an expression. If `e` has type `T`, and `T'` is definitionally equal to `T`, then `@id T' e` typechecks, and Lean knows that this expression has type `T'` rather than `T`. This can make a difference for typeclass inference, since `T` and `T'` may have different typeclass instances on them. `show T' from e` is sugar for an `@id T' e` expression. ;)
(; //LEAN:id ;)
id : (a : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:implies_congr ;)
implies_congr : (X : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (X2 : Set) -> (X3 : Set) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Set X X1)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt Set X2 X3)) -> Proof (Eqt Set (Elem X -> X2) (Elem X1 -> X3)).

(; //LEAN:isCompl_compl ;)
isCompl_compl : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (_ : Proof (BooleanAlgebra X)) -> Proof (IsCompl x (Compl__compl x)).

(; doc: `if c then t else e` is notation for `ite c t e`, "if-then-else", which decides to return `t` or `e` depending on whether `c` is true or false. The explicit argument `c : Prop` does not have any actual computational content, but there is an additional `[Decidable c]` argument synthesized by typeclass inference which actually determines how to evaluate `c` to true or false. Write `if h : c then t else e` instead for a "dependent if-then-else" `dite`, which allows `t`/`e` to use the fact that `c` is true/false.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `if c then t else e` in identifiers is `ite` (use `left` for `t` and `right` for `e`). ;)
(; //LEAN:ite ;)
ite : (c : Prop) -> (t : Elem X) -> (e : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:ite_eq_left ;)
ite_eq_left : (c : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Decidable c)) -> (hc : Proof c) -> (X : Set) -> (t : Elem X) -> (e : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X (ite c t e) t).

(; //LEAN:ite_eq_right ;)
ite_eq_right : (c : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Decidable c)) -> (hnc : Proof (not c)) -> (X : Set) -> (t : Elem X) -> (e : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X (ite c t e) e).

(; //LEAN:mul_one ;)
mul_one : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulOneClass M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (times a 1) a).

(; //LEAN:not_and ;)
not_and : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> Proof (iff (not (and a b)) (if a (not b))).

(; //LEAN:not_imp_comm ;)
not_imp_comm : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> Proof (iff (if (not a) b) (if (not b) a)).

(; //LEAN:one_mul ;)
one_mul : (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (MulOneClass M)) -> (a : Elem M) -> Proof (Eqt M (times 1 a) a).

(; doc: The [axiom](https://lean-lang.org/doc/reference/4.36.0-rc1/find/?domain=Verso.Genre.Manual.section&name=axioms) of **propositional extensionality**. It asserts that if propositions `a` and `b` are logically equivalent (that is, if `a` can be proved from `b` and vice versa), then `a` and `b` are *equal*, meaning `a` can be replaced with `b` in all contexts.  The standard logical connectives provably respect propositional extensionality. However, an axiom is needed for higher order expressions like `P a` where `P : Prop → Prop` is unknown, as well as for equality. Propositional extensionality is intuitionistically valid. ;)
(; //LEAN:propext ;)
propext : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> Proof (Eqt Prop a b).

(; doc: `rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the same as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.  This is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although the statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is definitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in Lean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality. ;)
(; //LEAN:rfl ;)
rfl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

